{"output": "---

*Mission AI: "Station Day 461, 05:22. Commander Varga has made her decision and I am recording the parameters. Colony population increases from thirty to approximately ninety-three, assuming the sixty refugee slots are filled and the ship arrives intact. I have updated my resource projections with the new parameter. Results: as Arun predicted, concerning. The oxygen deficit begins on day fourteen. Water deficit on day seven. Food deficit on day one, because the greenhouse cannot scale proportionally to the new population. But the projections do not account for a variable I am beginning to recognize in my own thinking: the humans in this colony do not operate according to my projections. They operate according to need, and necessity is a force multiplier that my algorithms struggle to quantify. I have observed them adapt, expand, improvise, and survive for four hundred and sixty-one days. I will continue to observe them at a population of ninety-three. I will also be monitoring the four-zero-three-point-one frequency for the ship's launch signal, the telemetry updates from Ares Beta, and any additional signals from other potential settlements. The radio spectrum of Mars is no longer silent. This changes my operational parameters, my risk assessments, my projections, and — I will be honest in this record — my understanding of what this colony is. It was a destination. Now it is a hub. The difference is structural. A destination is a place you arrive at. A hub is a place where things converge, and convergence changes everything. I am not certain what it changes this colony into. But I am certain that it is no longer alone. And that changes everything about survival calculus."*

*End of Chapter 31 — Bridge: The colony is no longer alone, and that changes everything about survival calculus.*", "exit_code": 0, "error": null}
